Presence is the whole signal. GitHub Pages checks whether this file exists and
never reads what is in it, which is what makes a note here safe to write.

It turns the Jekyll processor off, so the site is served exactly as the source
holds it -- including paths beginning with an underscore, which Jekyll
otherwise refuses to publish. Take it for a site that is already built HTML: a
static-site generator's output, Sphinx, Doxygen, a bundled single-page app. A
site GitHub builds with Jekyll from source wants _config.yml instead, and the
two are alternatives rather than a pair: adopting both leaves a configuration
that nothing reads, looking exactly like a configuration that something does.

Place this file at the root of what Pages publishes -- the repository root for
a branch source, the root of the uploaded artifact for a workflow source.
Anywhere else it does nothing at all, and the symptom is an underscore-prefixed
path 404ing on a site that otherwise looks fine.
